onment availability are supplied inputs. These results illustrate prospective resolution **conditional on a supplied class and fixture**; they do not derive a universal hard switch or turn an amplitude margin into the Q156 Boolean predicate. The site's metric candidate is not promoted and calls for non-constructor review.\n\nPaper I states that affine event maps are exact **given receipts**, with their pre-event source unresolved. Paper IV implements a **supplied gate word** in a prepared controller and explicitly leaves the production-to-capture trigger and autonomous cycle open. Those papers are consistent with the current predictive-switch gap.\n\n## Replay and next evidence gate\n\nRun `python3 verify.py`. Exact Boolean/Fraction replay passes **13 named checks**: the four source strata, unique intact-null ambiguity, \\(2^m\\) across all three-pair strata, the conditional bridge's two remaining closed patterns, and Hodge-margin fixtures and symmetries. These are constructor checks of statements as read; they do not validate an unsourced cross-type bridge.\n\nThe concrete next source target is a typed map from pair \\((q,T,L)\\) and any retained-semantic gates to addressed edge/face \\((C_{\\rm cl}^{\\rm hard},T_{\\rm int}^{\\rm hard},h_f)\\), followed by a pre-event rule for the intact-null response bit and a causal receipt for the event address, update and time/order. Test that rule without using its post-event target as an input, including tied and simultaneous candidates. Until then, the live site adds a useful exact constraint and operational margins but **does not promote predictive Q156 hard-mask switching**. Physical promotion remains **0**.\n"
