    "content": "# UD explicit thirty seven preparation certificate\n\n**RESULT — Constructed 37 explicit rational preparations and certified that they excite all 1893 real observable directions of the original 62-channel shell.** Thirty seven is minimal for this free-evolution, independent-initial-state Hankel formulation. Finite uniformly sampled response matrices have certified nonzero condition bounds 15360, 3072 and 5120/3 at the three declared schedules. The original 747-dimensional unobservable subspace remains.\n\nPhysical promotion is **0**. This is constructor verification in the development lane with no registry entry; independent review is pending. The preparation and measurement contracts below are mathematical specifications, not records of a physical experiment.\n\n## Explicit preparation matrix\n\nLet S be the 2640 by 37 sign matrix generated by the supplied deterministic algorithm in `build_preparations.py`. Its SHAKE256 domain string, bit order, reshaping convention and resulting checksum are frozen in `PREPARATION_DESIGN.json`. The complete matrix is supplied in `PREPARATIONS.npz`.\n\n\\[\nX=S/52,\\qquad S_{ia}\\in\\{-1,+1\\},\\qquad a=1,\\ldots,37.\n\\]\n\nThe denominator 52 is the smallest integer at least the square root of 2640; it ensures that each preparation has norm at most one. It is a normalization choice, not a derived physical constant. Exactly,\n\n\\[\n\\|x_a\\|^2=\\frac{2640}{52^2}=\\frac{165}{169}<1.\n\\]\n\nAn exact Gram calculation gives maximum absolute row sum 4450 for `S^T S`, hence `||X||^2<=4450/2704<2`. Its rank modulo 1009 is 37, so the preparations are also linearly independent over the reals. Independence alone would not prove complete excitation; the modal checks supply that additional proof.\n\nAll 2640 global chain coordinates participate in each preparation. No hardware method for setting these amplitudes is assumed or certified.\n\n## Excitation proof and minimality\n\nLet A and R be the frozen nominal real generator and original 62-coordinate vertex-link observer. Write `L=-A^2`, with square-free polynomial q_L of degree 53. Its 53 distinct nonnegative roots give 105 signed A-frequencies, including zero. For each signed frequency lambda, let E_lambda be its spectral projector, and define\n\n\\[\nB_\\lambda=R E_\\lambda X\\in\\mathbb C^{62\\times 37}.\n\\]\n\nThe earlier exact modal rank table bounds the rank of each B_lambda by the observable multiplicity a_lambda. This audit evaluates the projectors using exact integer cross moments\n\n\\[\nG_k=R L^k S,\\qquad J_k=R A L^k S,\\qquad 0\\le k\\le 52.\n\\]\n\nThe preparation-side annihilator identity `S^T q_L(L)=0` and the zeroth moments are checked with arbitrary-precision integers. Positive roots are isolated by exact rational intervals narrower than 2^-400. At 512-bit interval precision, all 53 zero/positive-frequency blocks pass positive-eigenvalue tests; negative-frequency blocks are their conjugates. These prove\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}B_\\lambda=a_\\lambda,\n\\qquad \\sum_\\lambda a_\\lambda=1893,\n\\]\n\nwith uniform bounds on every nonzero singular value:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2^{-27}\\le\\sigma_{\\min,+}(B_\\lambda)^2,\n\\qquad\\|B_\\lambda\\|^2\\le 2^{-7}.}\n\\]\n\nThe interval algorithm encloses `B_lambda^* B_lambda`, then verifies the residual and Gram defect of an approximate unitary diagonalization. Numerical eigenvalues choose a basis only; interval inequalities certify the result. Exact modal upper ranks distinguish genuine zero eigenvalues from small positive ones.\n\nAt eigenvalue `+2i`, the observable complex eigenspace has dimension 37. With p initial preparations, time shifts only multiply its projected preparation vectors by scalars, so their span has dimension at most p. Consequently p must be at least 37. The supplied X achieves all modal ranks, establishing the minimum for this formulation. This is not a minimum for arbitrary driven-control experiments, state reconstruction with a known operator, or hardware measurement cost.\n\n## Finite sampling and conditioning\n\nUse normalized clock step `Delta=1/2`. For each preparation acquire\n\n\\[\ny_a(j)=R\\exp(jA/2)x_a,\n\\qquad j=0,\\ldots,2M-2.\n\\]\n\nDefine the normalized block response Hankel matrix\n\n\\[\nH_M[(i,c),(j,a)]=\\frac 1M\n\\left(R\\exp((i+j)A/2)x_a\\right)_c,\n\\qquad 0\\le i,j<M.\n\\]\n\nIt has 62M rows and 37M columns. The certified matrix is a finite-sample response Hankel matrix, not a matrix of high-order numerical derivatives. It factors as\n\n\\[\nH_M=(V_M\\otimes I_{62})\\,\n\\operatorname{diag}_\\lambda(B_\\lambda)\\,\n(V_M^T\\otimes I_{37}),\n\\]\n\nwhere `V_M[j,lambda]=exp(j lambda/2)/sqrt(M)`. The transpose on the right is intentional. The full matrix is real because conjugate frequency terms pair.\n\nThe inherited exact-frequency certificate bounds the reciprocal-sine sum by 1786. It gives temporal Gram spectrum within `[1-epsilon,1+epsilon]` on each axis, with epsilon respectively 7/8,1/2 and 1/4. Combining both temporal factors with the newly certified modal bounds yields\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\min,+}(H_M)^2\\ge(1-\\epsilon)^2 2^{-27},\n\\qquad\n\\|H_M\\|^2\\le(1+\\epsilon)^2 2^{-7}.\n\\]\n\n| M on each Hankel axis | Raw time vectors per preparation | Last normalized time | Rank | Nonzero condition bound | Scalar outputs |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2048 | 4095 | 2047 | 1893 | 15360 | 9393930 |\n| 4096 | 8191 | 4095 | 1893 | 3072 | 18790154 |\n| 8192 | 16383 | 8191 | 1893 | 5120/3 | 37582602 |\n\nThe matrix sizes are 126976 by 75776, 253952 by 151552 and 507904 by 303104. The certificates use the frequency factorization; these enormous dense matrices need not be formed. The data counts include all 37 preparations and 62 channels, without blanks or calibration repetitions. These are different experiments from the existing 260-preparation fault validation and the single-unknown-state 189-channel observation protocol; their costs cannot be compared as interchangeable tasks.\n\n## Bounded-error signal rank\n\nSuppose every acquired 62-component output vector differs from its declared exact response by Euclidean norm at most delta. Errors may be correlated across times and preparations. Repeated anti-diagonal entries in the Hankel matrix do not create independent data. Direct Frobenius counting gives\n\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde H_M-H_M\\|_2\n\\le\\|\\widetilde H_M-H_M\\|_F\n\\le\\sqrt{37}\\,\\delta.\n\\]\n\nLet s be the declared lower bound on the smallest nonzero nominal singular value. The following exact output-vector budgets imply `sqrt(37) delta <= s/4`:\n\n| M | Total error ceiling per 62-component raw vector | Condition bound on retained 1893 signal values |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2048 | 2^-22 | 61441/3 |\n| 4096 | 2^-20 | 12289/3 |\n| 8192 | 2^-19 | 20483/9 |\n\nWeyl bounds place the first 1893 singular values at or above `3s/4` and all remaining values at or below `s/4`. Thresholding at `s/2` therefore returns 1893 signal directions. The noisy matrix may have higher algebraic rank; that rank is not claimed to remain 1893.\n\nThe displayed retained condition bounds follow from `(4 kappa+1)/3`. A sufficient nominal-model allocation is preparation error at most `delta/4`, additive readout-vector error at most `delta/4`, and normalized time jitter at most `delta/16`: because `||A||<4` and `||x_a||<1`, the three contributions sum to at most `3delta/4`. This is a candidate instrument specification, not a measured capability. Generator, metric or persistent readout errors require either a separate bound or inclusion in the total raw-response error contract.\n\n## Interpretation and limits\n\nThis construction replaces the previous generic-existence assertion with an explicit minimal preparation set and a finite-sample signal-conditioning certificate. It excites the full 1893-dimensional observable quotient. It does not remove the original 747-dimensional dynamical kernel, reconstruct its hidden states, identify a gauge group, or calibrate physical time.\n\nThe characteristic 13 result remains separately rank 1877 with kernel 763 for the canonical reduction. These real preparations and interval bounds are not transferred to that field. The coefficient ledger's Q139 physical-clock nonidentifiability remains open. The supplied governance subdivision remains a proposal; this capture makes no registry promotion.\n\n`validate_preparations.py` replays the exact design, integer moments, refined roots and interval certificates in an isolated directory. Constructor replay does not constitute non-constructor review. The next operational task is to turn the total response-error budgets into independently tested preparation, readout and clock contracts.\n"
